apiVersion: meta.pkg.crossplane.io/v1
kind: Provider
metadata:
  name: provider-{{providerName}}
  annotations:
    # NOTE: this block IS the Marketplace listing. Everything here is rendered
    # publicly, so keep it accurate.
    meta.crossplane.io/maintainer: {{gitUserId}} (https://{{gitHost}}/{{gitUserId}})
    meta.crossplane.io/source: {{gitHost}}/{{gitUserId}}/{{gitRepoId}}
    meta.crossplane.io/license: Apache-2.0
    meta.crossplane.io/description: |
      Manage {{providerTitle}} Identity Server as Kubernetes resources.
    meta.crossplane.io/readme: |
      `provider-{{providerName}}` manages {{providerTitle}} Identity Server as Kubernetes
      resources, so tenants, organizations and applications can be declared
      alongside the rest of your cluster and reconciled by Crossplane.

      It is GENERATED from the API descriptions {{providerTitle}} publishes with the
      server itself, so what it can manage is what those documents describe --
      no more, and nothing hand-maintained that can drift from them.

      ## Managed resources
{{#apiInfo}}{{#apis}}{{#operations}}

      * `{{kind}}`
{{/operations}}{{/apis}}{{/apiInfo}}

      ## Getting started

      Install the provider, then give it an endpoint and a token.
      {{providerTitle}} has no OAuth grant a controller can run -- its server
      enables the authorization-code and refresh-token grants and nothing else
      -- so what it takes is a long-lived access token issued to an API user,
      not a client to mint one with.

      ```yaml
      apiVersion: pkg.crossplane.io/v1
      kind: Provider
      metadata:
        name: provider-{{providerName}}
      spec:
        package: xpkg.upbound.io/{{gitUserId}}/provider-{{providerName}}:v0.1.0
      ---
      apiVersion: v1
      kind: Secret
      metadata:
        name: {{providerName}}-token
        namespace: crossplane-system
      type: Opaque
      stringData:
        token: "..."
      ---
      apiVersion: {{groupName}}/{{apiVersion}}
      kind: ClusterProviderConfig
      metadata:
        name: default
      spec:
        endpoint: https://hr.example.com/web/index.php
        credentials:
          source: Secret
          secretRef:
            namespace: crossplane-system
            name: {{providerName}}-token
            key: token
      ```

      The endpoint is on the spec rather than in the secret because it is not
      one -- and because the token is a bare string, so a Secret something else
      already made can be pointed at as it is. A credentials blob that is a JSON
      document may carry `endpoint` and `token` instead.

      See the `examples/` directory in the source repository for a manifest per
      managed resource.
spec:
  # SafeStart, NOT "safe-start". crossplane/provider-template ships the
  # kebab-case spelling, which is not the enum value: Crossplane silently
  # prunes it, the ProviderRevision comes up with no capabilities, and the RBAC
  # manager therefore never grants the CRD get/list/watch that a safe-start
  # provider needs. The binary still runs its crd-gate, which then cannot sync
  # and the container CrashLoopBackOffs with "failed to wait for crd-gate
  # caches to sync".
  capabilities:
    - SafeStart
